                <pb facs="0870" n="744" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg744"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/6569: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-08-30T18:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 30,
                                1925—6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received August 30—10:55
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>365. Following are extracts from telegram received from Canton: <pb facs="0871"
                        n="745" xml:id="pg745"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“August 27, 6 p.m. City in complete control Whampoa Cadets and civil
                            administration badly disorganized. There was occasional firing Tuesday
                            night and yesterday but conditions now fairly quiet. Hsu Chung-chi,
                            leading Cantonese general, is supporting Reds but his allegiance is
                            doubtful. Bolshevik Russians seem to be very nervous but there is no
                            indication of further fighting unless Cantonese troops in the outlying
                            districts attack.</p>
                        <p>The Government now consists of provisional commission of three including
                            General Hsu Chung-chi, Wang Shao-ning and General Chiang, commander of
                            the Whampoa Cadets.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
